Subject: src/patch.c: on OpenVMS, plain file names and the name "patch"

safe.c walks every file name through directory descriptors (openat(),
fchmodat(), ...), a defence against symbolic-link races.  On VMS those
are gnulib's emulation over open() of a directory, and setting the mode of
the patched temporary file failed with ENOENT, so patch -p1 into a
subdirectory stopped before renaming it.  Set unsafe, as patch already
does for a file named on the command line: plain file names, while
name_is_valid() still rejects absolute names and "..".

argv[0] is the image's file specification on VMS, which patch printed in
every message ("/DISK$USER/.../PATCH.EXE: ..."); call it "patch".

--- a/src/patch.c	2026-10-05 07:41:51.774290477 +0100
+++ b/src/patch.c	2026-10-05 07:41:51.789807869 +0100
@@ -160,7 +160,20 @@
     bool have_git_diff = false;
 
     exit_failure = EXIT_TROUBLE;
+#ifdef __VMS
+    /* argv[0] is the image's file specification; call it "patch" in
+       messages.  */
+    set_program_name ("patch");
+    /* safe.c walks each file name through directory descriptors (openat()
+       and friends), a defence against symbolic-link races; on VMS they are
+       gnulib's emulation, through which setting the mode of the output file
+       failed (ENOENT).  Use plain file names, as patch does for a file
+       named on the command line; name_is_valid() still rejects absolute
+       names and "..".  */
+    unsafe = true;
+#else
     set_program_name (argv[0]);
+#endif
     init_time ();
 
     xstdopen ();
